WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.890 --> 00:00:09.700
science certainly affects everything we
do, but it's also true that we affect the

00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:14.350
scientific process. Imagine that science
and society communicate through a game

00:00:14.350 --> 00:00:19.010
of telephone. Information is passed from
hand to hand, or institution to

00:00:19.010 --> 00:00:23.789
institution- you see, scientists published
research papers, which eventually reach

00:00:23.789 --> 00:00:27.820
policymakers, who then apply the
knowledge in ways that benefit the world.

00:00:27.820 --> 00:00:34.620
Right? Well, sort of. This picture gets
complicated once you realize that

00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:38.840
society creates a climate of social,
economic, and political factors that

00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:43.210
influence scientists and policymakers. The linear game of telephone becomes a

00:00:43.210 --> 00:00:48.870
loop, and the scientific message is more
likely to become distorted. Here, we can

00:00:48.870 --> 00:00:52.980
show you how this loop is formed by
telling the story of asbestos mining in

00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:59.570
South Africa. Wait, wait, wait. You've probably heard of asbestos, but do you really know

00:00:59.570 --> 00:01:00.859
what it is?

00:01:00.859 --> 00:01:06.060
Asbestos is a mineral that's stronger
than steel, inflammable, sound-absorbent,

00:01:06.060 --> 00:01:10.409
and flexible, which makes it an
economically important resource. Between

00:01:10.409 --> 00:01:15.420
1910 and 2002, South Africa was the
world's third largest exporter of

00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:17.869
asbestos. During that time,

00:01:17.869 --> 00:01:22.750
South Africa turned a huge profit, mining
more than 10 million tons of it- that's

00:01:22.750 --> 00:01:27.250
equivalent to almost 28 Empire State
Buildings! But let's take a closer look

00:01:27.250 --> 00:01:31.890
at the social context. In
twentieth-century South Africa, life was

00:01:31.890 --> 00:01:36.399
divided along racial lines of apartheid. The government enforced segregation of

00:01:36.399 --> 00:01:40.799
public spaces, medical care, even
citizenship. With limited employment

00:01:40.799 --> 00:01:45.649
opportunities, many Black South Africans
turned to manual work like asbestos

00:01:45.649 --> 00:01:50.969
mining. Entire families would work
together to drill, collect and separate

00:01:50.969 --> 00:01:55.350
asbestos from the earth. But asbestos, as it turns out, is particularly

00:01:55.350 --> 00:02:00.810
dangerous to mine. Although asbestos may look like solid rock, when it's drilled

00:02:00.810 --> 00:02:05.329
and blasted by miners, it can unravel
into millions of small fibers, which

00:02:05.329 --> 00:02:09.890
people can breathe in. Normally, when a
foreign substance enters the lungs, it

00:02:09.890 --> 00:02:15.069
gets digested into mucus and coughed up. But asbestos dust built up over time,

00:02:15.069 --> 00:02:17.870
eventually causing inflammation

00:02:17.870 --> 00:02:22.790
and the formation of scar tissue. Daily
exposure to asbestos may cause serious

00:02:22.790 --> 00:02:26.040
respiratory problems that appear later
in life.

00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:29.760
Illnesses can lay dormant for decades
within the body, such as mesothelioma,

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:33.940
which are cancerous tumors that
form in vital organs. Families of

00:02:33.940 --> 00:02:38.190
mine workers knew they were getting sick
from asbestos exposure, but the industry

00:02:38.190 --> 00:02:42.720
ignored their protests. As more people
found out about the dangers of asbestos,

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:47.019
how long do you think it took for mines
to start implementing workplace safety

00:02:47.019 --> 00:02:53.420
regulations? In 1959, scientists presented
extensive research demonstrating that

00:02:53.420 --> 00:02:58.220
mesothelioma was unusually common
in South African miners, and that even

00:02:58.220 --> 00:03:04.040
brief exposure to asbestos could cause
symptoms to appear. It wasn't until 1986

00:03:04.040 --> 00:03:07.859
that mining companies started
negotiating with miners to improve

00:03:07.859 --> 00:03:13.359
workplace safety-- it took over a quarter
of a century for this science to be

00:03:13.359 --> 00:03:17.599
implemented into policy. What happened
here to cause such a delay between

00:03:17.599 --> 00:03:21.569
science and action? Well, remember how
society shapes the game of telephone

00:03:21.569 --> 00:03:27.190
into a loop? Between 1959 and 1986,
South Africa's economic interests

00:03:27.190 --> 00:03:31.450
favored asbestos mining, further
distorting the scientific message. The

00:03:31.450 --> 00:03:35.919
1959 publication prompted the mining
industry to fund research on the health

00:03:35.919 --> 00:03:39.930
effects of asbestos, but this research
wasn't at all designed to examine the

00:03:39.930 --> 00:03:44.510
link between workplace conditions and
lung disease. For example, one study even

00:03:44.510 --> 00:03:49.470
attempted to link mesothelioma to a polio
vaccine that most South Africans hadn't

00:03:49.470 --> 00:03:54.079
even received. Meanwhile, the mines
continued business as usual while black

00:03:54.079 --> 00:03:58.180
laborers protested their unsafe working
conditions. In the mid -70s and

00:03:58.180 --> 00:04:02.440
early 80s, the asbestos trade slowed
as both global and domestic economic

00:04:02.440 --> 00:04:08.319
pressure against apartheid increased. In
1986, after a national anti-asbestos

00:04:08.319 --> 00:04:12.879
campaign and several more strikes, the
first health and safety requirements for

00:04:12.879 --> 00:04:17.750
the asbestos mining industry were
finally put into place. In the end, it

00:04:17.750 --> 00:04:23.000
took a 'perfect storm' of social, political
and economic factors for science done in

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:26.630
the lab to directly benefit workers in
the mine. Today,

00:04:26.630 --> 00:04:30.300
scientific discoveries are made at an
accelerated rate. All of the new

00:04:30.300 --> 00:04:31.320
information goes

00:04:31.320 --> 00:04:35.820
through a similar game of telephone. The
story of asbestos mining shows us that

00:04:35.820 --> 00:04:40.840
while society depends on informed
science, scientists also need reminding

00:04:40.840 --> 00:04:42.540
of social contexts.

00:04:42.540 --> 00:04:47.330
A study in 2008, for example, showed that
carbon nanotubes have a fiber shape

00:04:47.330 --> 00:04:52.110
similar to asbestos. When mice were
exposed to these nanotubes, they

00:04:52.110 --> 00:04:55.800
developed mesothelioma-like
symptoms. Billions of dollars are funding

00:04:55.800 --> 00:04:59.890
nanotechnology research, and carbon
nanotubes can already be found in a

00:04:59.890 --> 00:05:04.970
variety of products for sunscreen to
eyeglasses. In short, scientists have

00:05:04.970 --> 00:05:09.260
introduced promising material, but
ongoing research points to potentially

00:05:09.260 --> 00:05:13.080
harmful effects of its use. Sound
familiar?

00:05:13.080 --> 00:05:16.680
Science is a series of social
interactions, and especially when it

00:05:16.680 --> 00:05:17.630
comes to health,

00:05:17.630 --> 00:05:20.650
any conflicting messages in the
conversation can have serious

00:05:20.650 --> 00:05:24.910
consequences. People such as science
writers, professors and citizen

00:05:24.910 --> 00:05:29.260
scientists work against the likelihood
of these miscommunications! Moving

00:05:29.260 --> 00:05:33.520
forward, it is important to shorten the
gap between scientists and the public, to

00:05:33.520 --> 00:05:38.090
fortify the imperfect loop and keep
scientific messages connected to their

00:05:38.090 --> 00:05:39.230
social contexts!

